I’ll inspect the pattern, metadata, and the frozen framework guidance first, then identify candidate phases and build a GSAS‑II refinement that reproduces the submitted profiles and fractions. I’ll keep phase identification and quantitative refinement evidence separate so any ambiguity is explicit.

The chemistry points first to cobalt tungstate, but the raw pattern has a high, strongly sloping background and may contain residual or off‑stoichiometric phases. I’m now extracting peak positions and checking the local reference/indexing tools before committing to a phase inventory.

Peak positions now separate into two coherent families: monoclinic WO₃ accounts for the 23–24° triplet and 33–34° cluster, while wolframite‑type CoWO₄ accounts for the strong ~30.7° line and its higher‑angle series. I’m testing residual CoO/Co₃O₄ and alternative WO₃ polymorphs quantitatively; the phase count is not yet fixed.

The two-phase refinement is stable and already gives the right qualitative inventory: roughly 71 wt% monoclinic WO₃ and 29 wt% CoWO₄ at the scale-only stage. Unit-cell refinement markedly improves the fit; I’m now resolving strong calibration correlations and checking whether remaining residual peaks justify a third crystalline phase.

The monoclinic WO₃ model from COD 2311041 outperforms the other room-temperature/triclinic alternatives on the full profile. I’m retaining two phases—there are no independent residual peaks for CoO or Co₃O₄—and replacing the empirical common peak width with physically bounded, phase-specific size/microstrain terms before refining coordinates and texture.

The dominant remaining intensity correction is experimental, not a missing phase: refining polarization drives it to the physically allowed upper end and lowers Rwp from ~6.8% to ~6.1%. A common low Uiso for CoWO₄ is also strongly favored. I’m checking the last specimen effects (surface roughness and anisotropic broadening) before locking the fractions and structural coordinates.

The two-phase model is now well constrained: monoclinic WO₃ plus wolframite CoWO₄, with no convincing independent peaks from CoO or Co₃O₄. I’m doing the final consistency pass now—reconciling peak positions with the bounded physical profile model, then I’ll export the CIFs and verify every submitted file against the saved GSAS-II project.

The peak-position pass lowered \(R_\mathrm{wp}\) from 5.61% to 5.27% while keeping both monoclinic cells chemically sensible. I’m now applying the last physical constraints (nonzero displacement parameters and the stated roughness bounds) and testing only the heavy-atom coordinates that the X-ray data can actually support; oxygen coordinates will remain anchored to the reference structures unless the data justify movement.

The old CoWO₄ entry is a 1951 record with duplicated symmetry-equivalent sites and coarse oxygen coordinates. A modern wolframite analogue (ZnWO₄, COD 1520641; Zn²⁺ and Co²⁺ have nearly identical octahedral radii) gives a slightly better profile and a more defensible oxygen framework. I’m retaining that analogue framework, substituting Co, and refining only the two heavy-site \(y\) coordinates; the improvement is modest but chemically consistent with modern wolframites.

The final two-phase refinement now covers the complete 9.903–100.095° grid and gives \(R_\mathrm{wp}=4.985\%\). Explicit impurity tests returned CoO at only 0.10(9) wt% with a negligible fit change and Co₃O₄ at zero, so neither is defensible as a modeled phase. I’m writing the machine-readable result and the short uncertainty report, then I’ll do an independent replay/consistency check of the project, CIFs, fractions, and profile.

Completed the PXRD analysis.

- CoWO₄ (wolframite): 29.60 wt%
- Monoclinic WO₃: 70.40 wt%
- \(R_\mathrm{wp}=4.985\%\), \(R_p=3.802\%\)

Fractions are normalized over crystalline phases. No significant CoO or Co₃O₄ was resolved; approximately 0.3 wt% trace CoO cannot be excluded. Estimated systematic fraction uncertainty is ±1.5 wt%.

